Stop repainting entire layers when they merely change size.

Root cause of the load-time paint storm (device-proven on franceinfo.fr,
build ~459): one page load produced 598 Timeline Paint records totalling
~595 Mpx - 219 screenfuls rastered on CPU for a single page view - and a
gdb breakpoint on GraphicsLayerCoordinated::setNeedsDisplay() counted 572
whole-layer invalidations during that load. The two dominant sources:

1. GraphicsLayer::setSize() calls setNeedsDisplay() whenever
   shouldRepaintOnSizeChange() is true, and the coordinated port never
   overrides it (base impl: drawsContent()). But coordinated layers are
   backed by a tiled backing store (CoordinatedBackingStoreProxy): on
   resize, existing tiles keep valid content and only newly exposed tiles
   need painting - exactly why Apple's tiled layers skip this repaint.
   During a load the main content layer grows every time an image/ad/embed
   lands, so every growth repainted the whole page (2.7-4 Mpx a pass,
   dozens of passes).

2. RenderLayerBacking::updateGeometry() explicitly full-invalidates the
   scrolled-contents layer whenever the scrollable content size changes.
   Same story: the main frame's scrolled contents grow all through the
   load. Content whose rendering actually changed is already repainted by
   renderer-level dirty rects (repaintAfterLayout); the blanket repaint is
   redundant on grow. We keep it on shrink (edge tiles could hold stale
   pixels beyond the new content edge) and on padding-box offset changes
   (contents shift within the layer).

WEBKIT_REPAINT_ON_LAYER_RESIZE=1 restores stock behaviour for A/B.

